0227: decode / verify flash attention for a q8_0 K and a q4_0 V cache at head size 256, read in place (GGML_OPENCL_FA_DEC27=1, default off; unset, the dispatch is what it was). The dense 27B hybrid's 4 to 8-row attention calls (the 6-row verify of a 5-token draft) run flash_attn_gqa_dpas today: K and V are converted to f16 a 32-key tile into local memory, three barriers a tile, one work-group a core. kernels/flash_attn_dec27.cl takes the read forms of the FA port's q8_0:q4_0 build (0199q1 / 0225) to these calls: raw cache bytes by 2D block reads, codes and nibbles made f16 by integer operations, each block's scale in f32, no local memory, no barrier, Q as f16 through the DPAS as today. A sub-group owns 8 (query head, token) rows of a kv head and all 256 output columns (128 of the 256 registers); Q is re-read a key group from an f16 scratch a prepass writes (flash_attn_dpas2's form at this head size); the keys are split across threads so that one wave of the device is full (fa_dec27_gate.h: fa_dec27_splits - 32 splits at 6 rows on 160 compute units, 640 threads; a split walks 16 blocks at least), and a merge kernel joins the split states. fa_dec27_gate.h is the arm's pure gate (q8_0 K, q4_0 V, head 256, 4 to 8 rows, an f16 mask of one head, the strides the reads need, no sink / indexed bias / port sources / pages / ALiBi / softcap) and split rule. The dispatch asks the gate first, after the port's check: an accepted call is three launches (kernel_fa_dec27_q, kernel_fa_dec27, kernel_fa_dec27_merge); an 8-row block of a 9 to 64-row call (the row-block split) is not the arm's and keeps today's kernels (fa_dec27_gate_call, "row-block"); a call of the arm's kind that it does not serve is a DOWNGRADE line (dec27=declined reason=.., dec27=not-built ..) and keeps today's kernels. A TAKEN line, WARN once a process: "TAKEN src=opencl op=FLASH_ATTN dec27=in-place kv=q8_0:q4_0 head=256 grf=256 n_q=.. n_kv=.. kv_heads=.. tiles=.. mt=.. split=.. blocks=.. threads=.. wave=.. minblk=.. force=.. pv=norm". Knobs: GGML_OPENCL_FA_DEC27_SPLIT=N (the split count), _MINBLK=N (the least blocks a split, unset 16), _CU=N (the compute units of the wave), _TRACE=1 (a line a call), _RED=1..7 (red builds: V's scale from the next block, the last key dropped, a seam block a split lost, the merge without the maxima, K's scale twice, the P V phase without its 1 / c, the mask run block-read whatever its pointer), The P V phase has one form: the scale in P normalised a (32-key block, q4_0 block) by a power of two, pe = f16(p d c), O taken to that scale for the two DPAS and back in f32; offline its main kernel spills 3,648 bytes of scratch at 256 registers (no recompile). Two other forms were built and measured on the 24 GB card and are not in the series (the README's record): f16(p d 8) without the normalisation fails the V-floor case (4.9e-3; this form 3.4e-6), and P V in f32 without the DPAS is right and slower (1,793 against 1,186 us a 6-row call at 77,297 keys; today's kernels 2,811). The TAKEN line says pv=norm. fa_dec27_gate.h's slot numbers are checked against ggml-impl.h by static_asserts in the backend, and the gate looks at every source slot of the op (5 .. 19). get_proc_address "ggml_backend_opencl_fa_dec27_stats": the launches and the last one's split, for the tests. The dispatch takes the arm from FA_DEC27_MIN_KV = 8192 keys on: fa_dec27 has a fixed cost of ~153 us a call against ~50 us for flash_attn_gqa_dpas, independent of the keys; below 8192 keys the dispatch keeps today's kernel; at 4,096 keys the arm loses the 8-row case (+4.7 %), at 8,192 it wins at 4, 6 and 8 rows (-68 / -46 / -44 %); a shorter call is refused "below-crossover" (a ROUTE line once, no DOWNGRADE), GGML_OPENCL_FA_DEC27=2 forces the arm at every key count (the tests), and the TAKEN line carries min_kv. The mask run of a key group is a 16-half block read only where its pointer is on a 4-byte boundary, else a read a key: on the 24 GB card the first build's block read of an odd row over an odd key count (2 bytes past a boundary) gave the odd lanes the cell two before their own, and a call of 1,013 keys stood 7.0e-4 from the CPU; the served cache's key counts are multiples of 256, where every run is on 4 bytes.

